# Part Three — The Record: What Has Been Asked, 2016–2026

> The examiner has a memory, habits and a direction of travel. This part reads all three from eleven papers, so that you prepare for the paper that is likely, not the paper you fear.

## 3.1  How the record was built

Every descriptive question from CE-2016 to CE-2026 was taken from the past-paper volume in this folder. That is ==77 questions==. The five short-note questions (2017, 2018, 2019, 2024, 2026) were split into their parts, because each part is a separate subject: ==84 items== in all. Each item was then tagged four ways:

1. **Topic** — which of the seven topics it belongs to (its "home").
2. **Family** — the underlying question it asks, whatever the wording. "Write a comprehensive note on the autonomy versus integration debate" (2017) and "Discuss in detail how the autonomy and integration debate has contributed to the field" (2021) belong to the same family. There are ==48 families==.
3. **Command words** — define, differentiate, discuss, critically evaluate, and so on.
4. **Leaks** — any second topic the answer needs.

A full question counts as **one visit** to its topic; a short-note part counts as **half a visit**. The ==140 MCQs== recovered for 2016, 2018, 2019 and 2023–2026 were tagged by topic too. (The MCQ sheets for 2017, 2020, 2021 and 2022 could not be reliably recovered; no reconstruction has been attempted.)

## 3.2  The heat map

@py heatmap_topics

### How to read it

Each row is a topic, each column a paper. The darker the cell, the more the examiner visited that topic that year; the right-hand column is the total over eleven years. Three things stand out:

- **Two topics carry the paper.** T4 (gender and development, ==17.5==) and T1 (the discipline and social construction, ==16.5==) together account for ==44%== of all visits.
- **T4 never misses.** It appears in every paper, usually with one or two questions, and in 2019 with two and a half.
- **T1 has cooled.** After dominating 2016–2023, it fell to half a visit in 2024 and none in 2025. But its 2026 question — queer theory and the transgender law — shows it returning in a harder, contemporary form.

The same record, read by the nine syllabus heads rather than the seven topics, shows where inside each topic the pressure falls:

@py heatmap_heads

## 3.3  How much each topic weighs

@py topic_weights

The weights are unequal, but the lesson is not "study only the heavy topics". Look at the columns of the heat map: in most years, three or four different topics supply the seven questions. You need four good answers. A candidate who has mastered T1, T4 and T7 and neglected the rest will, in a typical year, find two or three questions they can answer well — not four. **Master every topic; master the heavy ones deeply.**

::: table | The seven topics ranked by weight.
@widths 1.4,6,1.8,1.8,5
Rank | Topic | Visits | Share | In how many of 11 papers
1 | T4 · Gender and Development | 17.5 | 22% | 11
2 | T1 · The Discipline & Social Construction | 16.5 | 21% | 10
3 | T7 · Gender-Based Violence & Case Studies | 10.5 | 13% | 9
4 | T3 · Feminist Movements | 9.5 | 12% | 8
5= | T5 · Status of Women in Pakistan | 8.5 | 11% | 7
5= | T6 · Gender and Governance | 8.5 | 11% | 9
7 | T2 · Feminist Theories | 7 | 9% | 7
:::

## 3.4  The repeat engine

The strongest predictor of the next paper is the last ten. The examiner returns again and again to a small number of families. Below, every family is listed by how often it has been asked.

### The families asked three times or more

@py repeat_engine 3

These fifteen families are the **core of the paper**. Between them they account for ==51 of the 84 items== — three in every five. Every one of them gets a full model answer in the QA documents, and most get more than one, because each repetition came with a different limb.

### The families asked twice

@py repeat_engine 2 2

### The families asked once

@py repeat_engine 1 1

### Exact and near repeats

The examiner does not mind repeating himself or herself word for word.

::: table | Exact and near repeats in the record.
@widths 3,3,8
First asked | Asked again | How close
2019 Q3 | 2023 Q5 | **Identical**: "Outline and explain the three major waves of feminist movements in the West. Discuss the influence of these waves on feminist movements in Pakistan."
2016 Q2 | 2023 Q2 | **Near identical**: a note on the status of Women's/Gender Studies in Pakistan, plus views on the autonomy vs integration debate.
2016 Q8 | 2021 Q7 | **Near identical**: critically review / analyse WID, WAD and GAD. (Asked again with two of the three in 2025 Q7.)
2016 Q3 | 2017 Q3; 2023 Q8 | **Same family**: the forms of violence against women in Pakistan and strategies to eliminate it.
2022 Q7 | 2023 Q6; 2024 Q3 | **Same family**: the colonial and capitalist perspectives of gender (2024 adds "development as coloniality").
2017 Q6 | 2018 Q6; 2019 Q5; 2025 Q3 | **Same family**: globalisation and gender inequality (2025 adds world-system theory).
2022 Q2 | 2023 Q3 | **Same limb**: the multidisciplinary nature of Gender Studies.
2019 Q4 | 2021 Q6 | **Same family**: modernisation theory and women's subordination.
:::

::: eye | What repetition means for you
The examiner is not short of ideas; he or she is testing the core. A family asked four or five times will be asked again. The question is never *whether* the waves, WID/WAD/GAD, the forms of violence, the women's movement or the quota will appear — only *in what form*. That is why the QA documents answer every repetition separately: the new limb (influence on Pakistan; "compare past and emerging trends"; "development as coloniality") is where the marks are.
:::

## 3.5  The leaks between topics

Many questions have a home topic but need material from another. The table lists every leak in the record. The Knowledge Bases cross-reference each other at exactly these points, so that no answer is ever built from one topic alone.

@py leaks_table

The strongest channels are **T5 ↔ everything** (Pakistan's data and laws feed almost every applied question), **T2 ↔ T1 and T5** (intersectionality), and **T3 ↔ T4** (the NGO-isation debate joins the movement to development).

## 3.6  The blind spots — syllabus lines never asked, 2016–2026

Some syllabus lines have never been the subject of a descriptive question. Blind spots are dangerous in two ways: candidates skip them, and examiners eventually fill them. The last three papers broke several long silences (world-system theory by name in 2025; queer theory in 2026), so expect more.

::: table | The blind spots of the descriptive paper.
@widths 3.3,5.2,5.5
Head | Syllabus line | Record
II | Historicizing Constructionism | Never by name (2016 Q6 asked for "theories of social construction")
III | Liberal Feminism | Never on its own; only inside comparisons (2022 Q3 "two Western schools"). MCQ 2023
III | **Men's Feminism** | **Never asked**, in either part of the paper
III | Psychoanalytic / Postmodern | Once each (2021 Q5; 2019 note)
IV | **United Nations Conferences on Women** | **Never a descriptive question** — though a favourite of the MCQs (2016, 2019, 2023, 2024, 2026)
IV | Third Wave on its own | Only paired with the first wave (2022) or within "the three waves"
V | World-System and Dependency theories | Only as partners of other theories (2019, 2025)
VI | **Status of Women in Education** | **Never a full question** — only through Malala (2025) and intersectionality (2026)
VI | Women and Law | Once directly (2020 Q6, human-rights quotation); partly in 2018 Q5
VII | **Defining Governance** | **Never asked**
VII | **Gender Issues in Women as Voters** | **Never asked** on its own
VII | Women as Candidates | Only within "obstacles faced by women politicians" (2024 Q7)
:::

::: eye | The most likely blind spots to break
Three blind spots stand out: **women and education** (a whole syllabus line, never asked, and at the centre of national debate — 25 million or more children out of school, most of them girls); **the UN conferences on women** (Beijing+30 in 2025 gives the examiner a perfect hook); and **women as voters** (the gender gap in the electoral rolls and the 10% turnout rule are rich, current material). Each is taught in full depth in its topic, and each is a strong candidate for the Prediction Papers.
:::

## 3.7  Beyond the syllabus — what the examiner asked that the syllabus never names

@py beyond_table

Eight items — ==10%== of the record — ask about something the syllabus does not name, and six of the eight come from the last two papers. The direction is unmistakable: the examiner reads the news, the reports and the recent literature, and expects you to have done so too. The contemporary layer of every Knowledge Base (§2.4) exists to meet exactly this demand.

## 3.8  The objective paper, 2016–2026

@py mcq_weights

@py mcq_table

### What the MCQs teach

- **The MCQ paper and the descriptive paper weigh the topics differently.** T4 dominates the descriptive paper but supplies only ==19== of 140 MCQs. T1 and T2 supply ==54== between them — nearly two in five — because the objective paper loves names, books and terms: who coined "hegemonic masculinity", who wrote *Gender Trouble*, what is androgyny.
- **The objective paper has turned theoretical.** In 2016, 2018 and 2019 most MCQs asked about Pakistan: laws, dates and "firsts" (the first woman Speaker, the first woman Foreign Secretary). From 2023, most ask about theory: performativity, standpoint theory, strategic essentialism, kyriarchy, the matrix of domination, Foucault's repressive hypothesis. In 2025 and 2026 together, ==12== of 40 MCQs came from T2 alone.
- **Defective items are common.** Several recent items had no correct option ("None of these" was the right answer to six 2026 items). Know the fact well enough to recognise when every option is wrong.

### MCQ facts asked more than once

