Women in Pakistan's first Constituent Assembly | Two (Begum Jahanara Shahnawaz and Begum Shaista Ikramullah) | 2016, 2024
First woman Speaker of the National Assembly | Dr Fahmida Mirza (2008) | 2016, 2019
Minimum marriage age for girls (federal law as then stood) | 16 years — **now 18 in Islamabad** under the 2025 Act | 2016, 2019
Androgyny | Having both masculine and feminine traits | 2023, 2024
Seneca Falls Convention | ==1848==; Elizabeth Cady Stanton and Lucretia Mott | 2024, 2025
Malala Yousafzai | Born ==1997==; works for girls' education | 2016, 2019
Patriarchal bargain | Deniz Kandiyoti (1988) | 2026 (twice in one paper)
:::

::: update | The child-marriage answer has changed
Two past MCQs (2016, 2019) keyed "16 years" as the minimum marriage age for girls under federal law — correct under the Child Marriage Restraint Act of 1929 as it then applied. The **Islamabad Capital Territory Child Marriage Restraint Act 2025** (assented 30 May 2025) sets ==18== for both girls and boys in the capital; Sindh has had 18 since 2013, and the Punjab Assembly passed a bill in April 2026. If the question asks for the *current* law, read it carefully: the answer now depends on the territory.
:::

## 3.9  How the questions are shaped

Beneath the wording, the eighty-four items come in seven shapes. Recognising the shape tells you the structure of the answer before you write a word.

::: table | The seven shapes of a Gender Studies question.
@widths 3.2,4.8,6
Shape | Example | The structure it needs
1. Define and differentiate | "Differentiate between Gender Studies and Women's Studies" (2023) | Define each; a table or list of contrasts on named criteria; examples; how they relate; conclusion
2. Explain a theory | "Discuss the development of Marxist/socialist feminism and its philosophical stance" (2021) | Origin and thinkers; core claims; key concepts; application to Pakistan; critique; conclusion
3. Critically evaluate | "Critically analyse WID and GAD … principles and limitations" (2025) | Define; principles of each; strengths; limitations; comparison; verdict
4. Pakistan-applied problem | "What is the status of women's health in Pakistan? How could it be improved?" (2017) | Data-led status; causes (with theory); consequences; measures; conclusion
5. Movement or history | "Trace the evolution of the feminist movement in Pakistan, from WAF to the digital activism of the 2020s" (2026) | Phases in order, each with events, leaders, laws and achievements; the shift the question names; evaluation
6. Case study | "Critically examine Malala Yousafzai's achievements and activism" (2025) | The person and the event; context; what she did; impact (local and global); criticism; lessons
7. Short notes | "(a) Is 'sex' also a social construct? (b) Radical feminism" (2024) | For each: definition, three to five developed points with evidence, one example, one-line conclusion — about 550–650 words
:::

## 3.10  The command words, decoded

@py command_words

::: table | What each command word demands.
@widths 3.4,10.6
Command | What the examiner wants
Define | The precise meaning, ideally with the source of the definition; then an example.
Differentiate / distinguish | The differences on stated criteria (a table is ideal), and a sentence on what the two share.
Discuss | Several sides of the issue, developed with evidence; your reasoned conclusion.
Explain / elaborate / elucidate | How and why something works; causes and mechanisms; examples.
Describe / outline | The main features in order, clearly; less argument, more coverage.
Trace | Development through time — phases, dates, turning points — in chronological order.
Critically analyse / examine / evaluate / review | The strengths *and* weaknesses, tested against evidence, ending in a judgement. Never only praise or only criticism.
Comment on (a statement) | Say whether and how far the statement is true, with evidence on both sides.
Give your views / in your opinion | A clear personal position — but argued with evidence, not asserted.
Compare | Similarities and differences, point by point.
What are / what is | Usually the first limb: definition plus a structured list; often followed by "how" or "why", which carries more marks.
Short notes | A compact, structured mini-answer; headings still help.
:::

## 3.11  What changed between 2016 and 2026

Read in order, the eleven papers fall into three eras.

::: fig flow | Three eras of the Gender Studies paper.
@per 3
2016–2019 · Foundations: define and differentiate; the discipline; WID/WAD/GAD; forms of violence; many short notes; MCQs on Pakistani laws, dates and "firsts"
2020–2023 · Depth: deconstructing gender; gendered language; psychoanalytic and Marxist feminism; SAPs; colonial perspectives; the case studies; MCQs turn to concepts
2024–2026 · Application: named laws and reports (PPWA 2016, Gender Gap 2025, Transgender Act 2018); intersectionality; climate; NGO-isation; the digital gap; MCQs on advanced theory
:::

Four shifts matter most for your preparation:

1. **From "what" to "how far".** Early questions asked what things are; recent ones ask you to evaluate, apply and critique. Description alone no longer scores.
2. **From the syllabus to the world.** Recent questions name reports, laws and statistics from the previous year or two. You need current facts, not only textbook theory.
3. **From single topics to combinations.** 2026 Q2 combines social construction, queer theory and Pakistani law; 2026 Q6 combines the movement with a development critique. Leaks are increasing.
4. **Longer question stems.** Several 2026 questions run to four or five lines, giving you facts to use (for example 2026 Q8(a) supplies the mobile-gap figures). Read them twice: the stem often contains the structure of the answer.

## 3.12  Where the next paper could come from

No one can predict a paper. But the record makes some questions much more likely than others. The tiers below guide how deeply to prepare each area. **Every syllabus line is covered in the Knowledge Bases whatever its tier**; the tiers decide how much practice-writing each deserves.

### Tier 1 — prepare to full-answer depth from every angle

::: table
@widths 4.2,1.1,8.7
Area | Topic | Why, and the angle most likely next
Gender-based violence: forms, causes, theories, laws and strategies | T7 | Asked in 9 of 11 papers (ten items). Next angle: the new domestic-violence laws (Islamabad 2025); online violence; masculinities
WID, WAD, GAD (with Moser and Kabeer) | T4 | Five visits. Next angle: gender mainstreaming and its critics; GAD in Pakistan's programmes (BISP)
The waves of feminism and Pakistan | T3 | Five visits, including an exact repeat. Next angle: the fourth wave and digital feminism
The women's movement in Pakistan | T3 | Six items (with APWA/WAF and NGO-isation), two in 2026 alone. Next angle: Aurat March; NGO-isation; the backlash
Colonial, capitalist and development theories; globalisation | T4 | Twelve items in nine papers, across five families. Next angle: gendered globalisation and platform work; IMF programmes
Social construction, sex/gender, masculinities and queer theory | T1 | Seven items in seven papers. Next angle: the transgender law and the courts; masculinities
Political quotas and participation | T6 | Six items in six papers. Next angle: the 2024 elections; the 5% ticket rule; reserved seats disputes
Intersectionality | T2/T5 | Asked in both of the last two papers — a new favourite
:::

### Tier 2 — prepare to a full plan and a strong short answer

